Question 11) Print all Subsequences -> A contiguous | non-contiguous sequences , which follows the order.

for any array with n size , total number of subsequences will be 2^n

🔍 Example (n = 3)

Array: [1, 2, 3]

Subsequences:

[]
[1]
[2]
[3]
[1,2]
[1,3]
[2,3]
[1,2,3]


👉 Total = 8 = 2³

using power set 
code: class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> subsets(int[] nums) {
        ArrayList<List<Integer>> list = new ArrayList<>();
        int n = nums.length;
        int outer = 1<<n;
        for(int i=0;i<outer;i++)
        {
            List<Integer> sub = new ArrayList<>();
            for(int j=0;j<n;j++)
            {
                if((i & (1<<j)) != 0)
                {
                    sub.add(nums[j]);
                }
            }
            list.add(sub);
        }
        return list;
    }
}

Using recursion: here is the approach , for every index either take or not take

public class practice{
    static int[] arr = {3,1};
    static void f(int i,ArrayList<Integer> list)
    {
        if(i>=arr.length)
        {
            System.out.println(list);
            return;
        }
        // Take or pick the particular index into the subsequence
        list.add(arr[i]);
        f(i+1,list);
        list.remove(list.size()-1);
        // not pick, or not take condition, this element is not added to your subsequence 
        f(i+1,list);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
        f(0,list);
    }
}

T.C. -> For every index we have 2 options , so time complexity will be definitely 2^n
S.C. -> O(N)

