Combination Sum II
====================

Brute Force Approach: bas ek baar bhi repeat nhi karna hai toh bas ek f(i+1) change kar denge baki pura code, almost same jayega like Combination Sum I
====================

code: class Solution {
    public void f(int i,int[]arr,int target, ArrayList<Integer> list,List<List<Integer>> ans)
    {
        if(i==arr.length)
        {
            if(target==0)
            {
                ArrayList<Integer> temp = new ArrayList<>(list);
                if(!ans.contains(temp))
                {
                    ans.add(temp);
                }
            }
            return;
        }
        if(arr[i]<=target)
        {
            list.add(arr[i]);
            f(i+1,arr,target-arr[i],list,ans);
            list.remove(list.size()-1);
        }
        f(i+1,arr,target,list,ans);
    }
    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum2(int[] candidates, int target) {
        Arrays.sort(candidates);
        ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
        List<List<Integer>> ans = new ArrayList<>();
        f(0,candidates,target,list,ans);
        return ans;
    }
}

Brute Force Approach Gave TLE 
T.C. -> O(2^t into k into logn)

The interviewer will not be happy with extra logn , so we have to bit modify our recursion in a bit different way

Optimal Approach: iska jo approach hai: isme hum kya kar rahe hai ki jo duplicate recursion calls ho rahe the usko hone nhi denge , isi liye tle yaha pe manage hojayega , recursion sirf unique ke liye call hi hoga , duplicate ke liye ignore hojayega

code: class Solution {
    public void f(int i,int[] arr,int target,List<List<Integer>> ans,List<Integer> list)
    {
        if(target==0)
        {
            ans.add(new ArrayList<>(list));
            return;
        }
        for(int j = i;j<arr.length;j++)
        {
            if(j>i && arr[j] == arr[j-1]) continue;
            if(arr[j]>target) break;
            list.add(arr[j]);
            f(j+1,arr,target-arr[j],ans,list);
            list.remove(list.size()-1);
        }
    }
    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum2(int[] candidates, int target) {
        List<List<Integer>> ans = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
        Arrays.sort(candidates);
        f(0,candidates,target,ans,list);
        return ans;
    }
}

Time Complexity: Assuming  all the elements are unique , Number of different combinations and subsequences the array generate  is 2^n , ans.add(new ArrayList<>(list)); (this line is not constant operation ) . Assuming that average length of combination is k . 
Final TC:        2^n into K
Space Complexity: k into x {Average length is k and assuming there are x combinations} ignoring auxiliary space used by recursion    









